// ---------------- The curriculum, part seven: the long climb (directing, writing, camera, editing, producing, acting) ----------------
// More lessons for the core crafts, so that mastering one takes years of school, sets and reading rather than a season.
// Same shape as the rest: [year, title, the idea, the technique, [a situation, [choice, skill, result], [choice, skill, result]], example].
// Appended after the existing lessons so saved lesson ids keep their numbers.
const CURR_LONG = {
  dir: [
    [1, "Coverage, and why you need less of it than you think", "Coverage is the set of angles shot so a scene can be cut together: a master, singles, overs, inserts. Shooting everything protects you but eats the day and tells the editor you didn't know what the scene was.", "Before the day, decide which shot carries each beat. Shoot that shot first while the actors are fresh, then cover only what the cut needs to reach it.", ["You're losing light with two set-ups left.", ["Shoot the shot the scene depends on", "vstory", "The scene works; the editor has fewer choices and doesn't need them."], ["Grab two fast, wide masters", "stag", "Everything is covered, and nothing is good."]], ""],
    [1, "Blocking before lensing", "Block the actors first, then place the camera. A camera that is set before the actors move forces them into marks that feel false; a camera that follows good blocking finds the scene.", "Rehearse with the actors and only the DP watching. Let the movement settle. Then talk shots, with the blocking already proved.", ["The DP wants to light before rehearsal to save time.", ["Rehearse first, even a short one", "stag", "The lighting fits the scene and nothing has to be relit."], ["Let them light, then fit the actors in", "pace", "Faster this morning, slower all afternoon."]], ""],
    [1, "Eyelines and the geography of a room", "Where an actor looks tells the audience where everyone else is. Mismatched eyelines across a cut make a room feel like it's been rebuilt between shots.", "Keep a floor plan with every actor's position and the camera's. On a single, put the off-camera actor's head just beside the lens, at the right height.", ["The other actor has gone home; you still need their close-up's eyeline.", ["Place a mark at the right height and side", "cont", "Seamless."], ["Have the actor look roughly that way", "dact", "It cuts badly; the editor flags it."]], ""],
    [1, "The table read", "A read-through with the cast and heads of department shows you what's funny, what's long and what doesn't land, while it's still cheap to change.", "Listen more than you speak. Mark every place the room went quiet, laughed or fidgeted. Talk to the writer that night, not in the room.", ["A scene everyone loved on paper dies at the read.", ["Rework it with the writer before the shoot", "vstory", "It plays on the day."], ["Trust it will be different on set", "tone", "It isn't."]], ""],
    [1, "Directing extras", "Background artists make a world feel real or fake. Left alone, they mime silently and cross the frame at the same moment as the lead.", "Give each group a reason to be there and a simple action. Stagger crosses. Ask for murmured, real conversation rather than silent mouthing, then let the mixer decide.", ["A busy café looks staged.", ["Give each table a story and a timing", "stag", "It feels lived in."], ["Ask everyone to just act natural", "dact", "Thirty people pretending to drink coffee."]], ""],
    [2, "Tone meetings", "Before prep goes deep, a director meets every department to agree what the film feels like: references, palettes, the music it is closest to, and the films it must not be.", "Bring a short reel of clips and stills and three adjectives. Ask each head of department what those mean for their work, and write down their answers.", ["Costume and design are pulling in different directions.", ["Call a joint meeting with the references", "tone", "One film again."], ["Let them each do their best work", "vis", "Two good films, sharing a frame."]], ""],
    [2, "Directing children", "Children can't hold a performance through forty takes and work shorter legal hours. The best child performances come from play, not instruction.", "Turn scenes into games. Keep takes short and early. Shoot their coverage first. Never let an adult talk down to them on set.", ["A seven-year-old freezes in front of the crew.", ["Clear the set and make it a game", "dact", "The take is alive."], ["Give a precise line reading", "tone", "The child repeats it like a parrot."]], ""],
    [2, "Directing animals", "Animals do what they want. Every animal shot needs a trainer, a plan that works with behaviour, and an editor ready to build performance from fragments.", "Ask the trainer what the animal does naturally and write the shot around that. Shoot more and shorter, and cover the humans so you can cut away.", ["The dog won't hit the mark.", ["Move the mark to where the dog goes", "setm", "Done in two takes."], ["Keep trying until it learns", "pace", "An hour gone and the dog is bored."]], ""],
    [2, "Night exteriors", "Nights are slow, cold and expensive. Lighting a street takes hours, crews tire, and turnaround rules eat the next day.", "Group all nights together in the schedule. Pre-light while it's still dusk. Choose a few big sources for the whole street rather than lighting every shot.", ["You have one night for a chase across four streets.", ["Simplify to two streets lit well", "setm", "It looks like a film."], ["Light all four thinly", "light", "It looks like a car park."]], ""],
    [2, "Weather as a scene partner", "Rain, wind and sun change performances and continuity. Real weather rarely cooperates; created weather is expensive but repeatable.", "Have a cover set ready for every exterior day. If you must shoot in changing weather, choose scenes where the change can be part of the story.", ["It starts raining halfway through a scene set in sunshine.", ["Move to the cover set and come back", "setm", "Continuity holds."], ["Keep shooting and fix it later", "cont", "The rain comes and goes between cuts."]], ""],
    [2, "Directing dialogue scenes that move", "Two people talking can be static or electric. The difference is usually blocking: who stands, who moves away, who closes the distance, and when.", "Map the power in each beat. Give the character who is winning the space; make the loser retreat or hold still. Change positions when the power changes.", ["A long kitchen argument feels flat.", ["Restage it around who's winning each beat", "stag", "It plays like a fight."], ["Cut it faster", "rhythm", "Shorter, still flat."]], ""],
    [2, "Inserts and close details", "An insert of a hand, a letter or a gun can carry more story than a page of dialogue. Directors often leave them to a second unit and lose control of the film's emphasis.", "List every story-critical detail in the script. Decide how each is revealed, and shoot the ones that matter yourself.", ["The insert of the ring is left to the last ten minutes.", ["Shoot it properly, lit and planned", "vstory", "The audience notices the ring exactly when they need to."], ["Snatch it on a phone lens", "comp", "It looks like an afterthought."]], ""],
    [2, "Working with a first AD", "The first AD runs the floor and the schedule so you can direct. A good partnership means the director decides what and the AD decides when.", "Tell your AD each morning which shots matter most and where you're willing to lose time. Never undermine them in front of the crew.", ["The AD warns you're two hours behind.", ["Cut a shot you can live without", "setm", "Back on schedule, scene intact."], ["Push on and hope", "pace", "You lose the scene at the end of the day instead."]], ""],
    [2, "Rehearsal in prep", "Rehearsal before the shoot lets actors find the relationships and lets you fix the script. Too much, and it goes stale before the camera turns.", "Rehearse the relationships, not the scenes. Talk, improvise around the story, run a few key scenes once, then stop.", ["The cast want to run every scene twice.", ["Run the hardest three and leave the rest fresh", "dact", "They arrive on set curious."], ["Run everything", "dact", "It is perfect in rehearsal and tired on the day."]], ""],
    [3, "The oner", "A long take with no cuts commits the audience to real time and builds tension cuts can't, but every department must be perfect at once and you can't fix anything in the edit.", "Rehearse it like theatre. Build hidden cut points in case. Decide in advance how many takes you'll go to before falling back on coverage.", ["Take twelve of a three-minute oner, and something always goes wrong.", ["Use the planned hidden cut", "vstory", "Nobody sees the join."], ["Go again, and again", "pace", "Take twenty-six is perfect, and the crew is in pieces."]], ""],
    [3, "Directing the edit", "The director's cut is the director's chance to find the film the shoot actually made. The film you planned and the film you shot are rarely the same.", "Watch the assembly once without stopping. Then cut toward what's alive in the footage, even if it means losing scenes you loved.", ["Your favourite scene stops the film dead.", ["Cut it", "vstory", "The film breathes."], ["Trim it and keep it", "rhythm", "Shorter, still in the way."]], ""],
    [3, "Test screenings", "Recruited audiences fill in cards and talk in focus groups. Their scores move release dates and budgets; their notes rarely say what's really wrong.", "Ignore the suggestions and look at the symptoms: where they fidgeted, what confused them, which character they cared about. Fix causes, not complaints.", ["The audience says the ending is too sad.", ["Find where the sadness wasn't earned", "vstory", "The same ending, and now it lands."], ["Shoot a happier ending", "mkt", "The scores go up; the reviews go down."]], ""],
    [3, "Reshoots and pickups", "Most films come back for pickups: inserts, fixes, the odd new scene. Planned well they save the film; planned badly they show.", "Plan reshoots from the cut. Match wardrobe, hair, lenses and light from continuity notes. Write anything new to the actors' availability.", ["An actor has changed their hair since the shoot.", ["Wig and careful framing", "cont", "Invisible."], ["Hope nobody notices", "tone", "Everyone notices."]], ""],
    [3, "Choosing your collaborators", "A director's most important decisions are made in hiring: the DP, the designer, the editor, the composer. Good collaborators argue with you and make the film better.", "Watch their work, then talk about yours. Hire the person who asks the best questions, not the one who agrees with everything.", ["The best DP available disagrees with your look.", ["Hire them and have the argument", "vis", "The film is better than either of your plans."], ["Hire someone who agrees", "tone", "Easy prep, ordinary pictures."]], ""],
    [3, "Directing on a franchise", "Big studio films come with a release date, a shared universe, notes from many departments and a second unit larger than most films. The director's job is protecting one clear idea.", "Pick the one thing your instalment is about and repeat it in every meeting. Give your second unit director a written brief with references.", ["The studio wants a new set piece added in post.", ["Find a version that serves your story", "pack", "They get their scene; you keep your film."], ["Refuse", "tone", "They shoot it without you."]], ""],
    [3, "A director's voice", "The directors people remember have recognisable concerns and a way of seeing that runs across films. A voice isn't a style laid on top; it comes from caring about the same things.", "Look at your own work and list what keeps coming back: a kind of character, a place, a question. Choose your next project because of it.", ["You're offered a well-paid job that isn't you.", ["Take it and find what's yours in it", "vis", "A film only you would make."], ["Take it and do it straight", "pack", "Money in the bank, nothing on the reel."]], ""]
  ],
  wri: [
    [1, "Scene description that reads fast", "Readers skim action lines. Big blocks of description get skipped, and with them the things you needed them to see.", "Keep paragraphs to three or four lines. One image per line. Write in the present tense and the active voice, and cut camera directions.", ["Your opening page is one dense paragraph.", ["Break it into short visual beats", "struc", "It reads like a film."], ["Leave it; it's all important", "orig", "The reader skims and misses the clue."]], ""],
    [1, "Character introductions", "The first line about a character is the reader's first impression and the casting director's first note. A list of physical traits says nothing.", "Introduce characters by what they do or how others react to them, in one or two lines: age, an essential quality, an action.", ["You've described your lead's hair and eyes.", ["Replace it with what she's doing", "char", "The reader knows her at once."], ["Add more detail", "orig", "Now we know her shoe size too."]], ""],
    [1, "Want and need", "A character's want is what they chase; their need is what they actually lack. Stories run on the gap between the two.", "Write both in a sentence each. Make sure the climax forces a choice between them.", ["Your hero gets everything she wants and the ending feels empty.", ["Make her choose between want and need", "char", "The ending means something."], ["Give her more to win", "struc", "Louder, still empty."]], ""],
    [1, "The inciting incident", "The event that knocks the hero's life out of balance. Too late and the reader is gone; too early and we don't care yet.", "Find it, then check how many pages before it. In most features, somewhere in the first fifteen.", ["Your inciting incident is on page 31.", ["Move the setup into fewer pages", "struc", "The story starts sooner and you lose nothing."], ["Keep the setup; it's character", "char", "The reader stopped at page 12."]], ""],
    [1, "Reading scripts", "Writers learn most by reading produced scripts alongside the films, noticing what was cut, what was invented on set and what the page did that the film couldn't.", "Read one script a week. For each, write down the first ten pages' events and where the act breaks land.", ["You haven't read a script in months.", ["Read three this week", "struc", "You see your own pages differently."], ["Keep writing instead", "orig", "More pages of the same habits."]], ""],
    [2, "Exposition hidden in conflict", "Information delivered calmly is boring. Information fought over is drama.", "Find the character who doesn't want the information said, and make someone say it anyway.", ["A scene explains the heist plan to the audience.", ["Have two crew members argue about the plan", "dial", "We learn it and enjoy learning it."], ["Use a voice-over", "struc", "Clear, and dull."]], ""],
    [2, "Set-ups and pay-offs", "Something planted early and returned to later gives an audience the pleasure of recognition. Plants that never pay off feel like mistakes; pay-offs that were never planted feel like cheats.", "List every pay-off. For each, find where it's planted and whether the plant is clear but not obvious.", ["The final twist relies on a key nobody saw.", ["Plant the key in an earlier, busy scene", "struc", "The twist feels inevitable."], ["Explain it after", "dial", "The audience feels cheated."]], ""],
    [2, "Subplots that rhyme", "A good subplot echoes the main story's theme from another angle. A subplot that doesn't is just running time.", "Write the main story's question in a line. Check every subplot answers it differently.", ["The comic-relief subplot is fun but unrelated.", ["Bend it to mirror the theme", "struc", "It's still funny, and it means something."], ["Keep it as a break", "comic", "The film loses its grip there."]], ""],
    [2, "Writing for a budget", "Every scene has a price. Night, crowds, cars, children, animals, water and period all cost money. Writers who know this get made.", "Mark expensive elements on a copy of the script. Ask which ones the story needs and how to get the same effect cheaper.", ["Your opener is a crowded stadium at night.", ["Find the same drama in a small space", "adapt", "Cheaper and more intense."], ["Keep it; it's the hook", "orig", "Nobody can afford to make it."]], ""],
    [2, "The second act problem", "Most scripts sag in the middle: the hero repeats the same kind of attempt and the stakes stop rising.", "Make a midpoint that changes the game: a reversal, a revelation, a false victory. Make each attempt after it cost more.", ["Pages 40 to 70 repeat the same chase.", ["Add a midpoint reversal", "struc", "The second half is a different film, in the best way."], ["Add another chase", "gcraft", "Longer, and the same."]], ""],
    [2, "Writing comedy", "Comedy is precision: the setup, the turn, the word at the end of the line. Jokes die on page if the rhythm is wrong.", "Put the funny word last. Cut every word between setup and punchline. Read it aloud to someone who doesn't love you.", ["A joke works out loud but not on the page.", ["Rewrite so the punch word lands last", "comic", "It plays on the page."], ["Explain it in the action line", "dial", "Now it's a description of a joke."]], ""],
    [2, "Genre conventions", "Every genre makes promises: the horror scare, the romcom meet-cute, the heist plan. Break them knowingly or keep them well; ignoring them confuses the audience.", "List the conventions of your genre. Decide which you'll honour and which you'll twist, and make the twist deliberate.", ["Your horror script has no scare for thirty pages.", ["Add a small scare that sets the rules", "gcraft", "The audience trusts the film."], ["Keep building dread", "tone", "The audience checks their phones."]], ""],
    [3, "The rewrite pass by pass", "Professional rewriting is done in passes, each with one job: structure, character, dialogue, polish. Trying to fix everything at once fixes nothing.", "List the notes. Group them by pass. Do structure first, because it changes everything after.", ["You've got notes on structure and a typo list.", ["Fix structure first", "struc", "The typos you'd have fixed are gone anyway."], ["Fix the easy things first", "dial", "A polished draft of the wrong story."]], ""],
    [3, "Taking notes", "Notes from producers and executives are symptoms, not prescriptions. 'Make her more likeable' may mean 'I don't understand why she does this'.", "Ask what the note is responding to. Find the cause, fix that, and you'll often satisfy the note without doing what it said.", ["An executive wants the villain to be nicer.", ["Ask what bothers them about him", "char", "He needs a reason, not niceness."], ["Make him nicer", "dial", "Now nobody's afraid of him."]], ""],
    [3, "Adapting a true story", "Real lives don't have three acts. Writers compress, combine people and invent scenes, which raises questions of truth and of law.", "Find the true thing the story is about and protect it. Use composite characters with care. Get the rights to life stories you lean on heavily.", ["The real events take ten years.", ["Compress around one turning year", "adapt", "A story, and still true."], ["Show all ten years", "struc", "A Wikipedia article with actors."]], ""],
    [3, "Staffing and the writers' room", "Television is written collectively. A room breaks stories on a board; individual writers draft episodes; the showrunner rewrites everyone.", "Pitch short and early. Build on other people's ideas. Never fight for your pitch past the second no.", ["Your idea is rejected, then the showrunner pitches it back as theirs.", ["Let it go and keep pitching", "cha", "You get the next episode."], ["Point out it was yours", "dial", "Correct, and remembered."]], ""],
    [3, "The spec script", "A spec is written without being paid, to sell or as a sample. A great spec gets meetings for years even if it never gets made.", "Write the script only you could write, in a genre that sells. Polish the first ten pages until they shine.", ["You're torn between a commercial idea and a personal one.", ["Write the personal one in a commercial shape", "orig", "It gets you in rooms."], ["Write what's selling right now", "gcraft", "So does everyone else."]], ""],
    [3, "Writing for a star", "A script built for a particular actor plays to their strengths and can get a film financed. Too tailored, and it collapses when they pass.", "Know what the star does best and give them scenes that use it. Keep the character strong enough to survive recasting.", ["The star passes and the role was written for their comic timing.", ["Rework the role around the character's need", "char", "It survives the next actor."], ["Wait for them to change their mind", "pack", "Two years go by."]], ""],
    [3, "Theme without speeches", "When characters state the theme, audiences feel lectured. Theme lives in the choices characters make under pressure.", "Delete any line that says what the film is about. Check the climax shows it instead.", ["The hero ends the film with a speech about forgiveness.", ["Have her forgive in an action", "char", "Silence, and a room in tears."], ["Make the speech shorter", "dial", "Less of a lecture."]], ""],
    [3, "Endings", "An ending must be both surprising and inevitable. Most are one or the other.", "Look back from the last scene. Does every earlier choice point toward it? Is there a version the audience didn't see coming?", ["The ending is exactly what everyone expected.", ["Find a cost the hero didn't see coming", "struc", "Surprising and earned."], ["Add a twist", "orig", "Surprising and unearned."]], ""],
    [3, "Selling the pitch", "A pitch is a performance: the story in ten to fifteen minutes, with the hook in the first breath and the ending clear. Executives buy enthusiasm and clarity.", "Open with why you have to tell this story. Tell it through the main character's eyes. Rehearse until you could do it in a lift.", ["You have twelve minutes and a forty-page treatment.", ["Tell it as a story with three big moments", "cha", "They lean in."], ["Read from the treatment", "struc", "They check the time."]], ""]
  ],
  cam: [
    [1, "Exposure: the triangle", "Aperture, shutter and sensitivity set exposure, and each has a side effect: depth of field, motion blur, noise.", "Fix the shutter at 180 degrees for natural motion. Choose aperture for the depth you want. Use light and ND filters to get there; raise sensitivity last.", ["A bright beach and you want a shallow focus look.", ["Add ND filters and open up", "light", "Soft background, correct exposure."], ["Raise the shutter speed", "move", "Shallow, but the motion stutters."]], ""],
    [1, "Lenses and perspective", "Wide lenses exaggerate depth and distort close faces; long lenses compress space and flatter faces. Lens choice changes how a scene feels.", "Choose focal length for the relationship between subject and background, not to fit the frame. Move the camera rather than zooming.", ["A claustrophobic scene in a big room.", ["A long lens, tight on faces", "comp", "The walls close in."], ["A wide lens to show the room", "comp", "Big, empty, the wrong feeling."]], ""],
    [1, "The camera report", "The camera assistant keeps a report of every roll or card: scene, take, lens, stop, filters, notes. Editors, VFX and insurance all rely on it.", "Fill it in at every take, not at the end of the day. Note anything unusual, like a flare or a bump.", ["You're behind and the report is blank.", ["Fill it in between set-ups", "cont", "VFX thanks you in six months."], ["Fill it in from memory at wrap", "speed", "Half the lens data is wrong."]], ""],
    [1, "Composition in thirds and breaking it", "The rule of thirds puts subjects off-centre with space to look into. Centred framing reads as formal, confrontational or strange.", "Leave look room in the direction the subject faces. Break the rule on purpose when a character is trapped or confronting the audience.", ["A character addresses the camera directly.", ["Centre her", "comp", "It feels like she's talking to us."], ["Keep her on a third", "comp", "It feels like she's talking to someone else."]], ""],
    [1, "Focus pulling", "The focus puller keeps the subject sharp as distances change, without looking through the lens. It is one of the hardest jobs on set.", "Measure and mark distances in rehearsal. Watch the actors, not the monitor. Agree with the DP who carries focus in a two-shot.", ["An actor misses their mark by a foot.", ["Follow the actor by eye", "speed", "Sharp."], ["Stay on your mark", "cont", "Soft, and take two."]], ""],
    [2, "Lighting faces", "Key, fill and back light shape a face. The ratio between key and fill decides mood: low for comedy, high for drama.", "Place the key on the far side of the face from camera (short side) for shape. Control fill with bounce or negative fill rather than more lamps.", ["A thriller scene looks like a sitcom.", ["Add negative fill and raise the ratio", "light", "Shadows, mystery."], ["Add a harder key", "light", "Brighter and still flat."]], ""],
    [2, "Motivated light", "Light on screen looks natural when it seems to come from a source in the scene: a window, a lamp, a television.", "Find the source in the set, then place your key to match its direction and colour. Keep it consistent across angles.", ["A bedroom scene with no visible lamp.", ["Add a practical lamp and motivate from it", "light", "Believable."], ["Light it beautifully from nowhere", "light", "Beautiful, and fake."]], ""],
    [2, "Camera movement with meaning", "A move should change the information or emotion in the frame. Moves for energy alone tire the audience.", "Begin and end each move on a meaningful frame. Decide what the move reveals or hides.", ["The director wants 'more energy'.", ["Find a move that reveals something", "move", "Energy with a reason."], ["Put it all on a gimbal", "move", "It drifts; nothing lands."]], ""],
    [2, "Shooting day for night", "Before digital, night was often shot in daylight and darkened, and it is still done for wide landscapes nobody can light.", "Shoot with the sun behind or to the side. Underexpose. Avoid sky in frame. Plan the grade with the colourist.", ["A desert night chase across miles.", ["Shoot day for night", "colour", "Convincing and affordable."], ["Try to light the desert", "light", "A small island of light and a big bill."]], ""],
    [2, "Multiple cameras", "A second camera saves time and catches unrepeatable moments but compromises lighting and lens choice.", "Give the B camera its own shot, usually a tighter or different angle on the same axis. Light for A; make B work.", ["A fight that can only be done twice.", ["Three cameras on different sizes", "speed", "Everything caught."], ["One camera, two takes", "comp", "Beautiful, and half missing."]], ""],
    [2, "Working with the gaffer and key grip", "The DP designs the light; the gaffer runs the electricians who place it; the key grip shapes and moves it. Good DPs explain intent, not lamp lists.", "Describe the look and the source. Let the gaffer propose the rig. Be clear about time.", ["Your gaffer proposes a different rig.", ["Hear it out", "light", "It's faster and better."], ["Insist on your plan", "light", "It works; an hour late."]], ""],
    [3, "Building a look", "A film's look combines lenses, filtration, lighting style, camera movement and the grade. It should come from the story, not a reference reel.", "Write one sentence about how the film should feel. Test lenses, stocks or LUTs against it with the actors in costume.", ["The director shows you twenty references, all different.", ["Find what they have in common", "vis", "A look of your own."], ["Copy the favourite", "comp", "Someone else's film."]], ""],
    [3, "Large format and anamorphic", "Bigger sensors and anamorphic lenses change depth, field of view and character: oval flares, shallow focus, wider frames.", "Test before committing. Remember focus is harder and lenses are heavier.", ["The producer asks why anamorphic costs more.", ["Show the test side by side", "pack", "They see it."], ["Explain technically", "comp", "They don't."]], ""],
    [3, "Virtual production volumes", "LED walls show backgrounds in camera, giving real reflections and light, but they need everything decided in advance.", "Lock the environment in prep. Match the foreground lighting to the wall. Watch for moiré on the panels.", ["The director changes the background on the day.", ["Explain the cost and find a cheap tweak", "pack", "A compromise in minutes."], ["Rebuild it", "speed", "Half a day lost."]], ""],
    [3, "Practical effects in camera", "Rain, smoke, fire and forced perspective in camera give actors something to play and look real in a way digital often doesn't.", "Plan with special effects and safety. Backlight rain and smoke. Shoot tests.", ["Rain is invisible on camera.", ["Backlight it", "light", "It pours."], ["Add more rain", "speed", "Wet actors, invisible rain."]], ""],
    [3, "Leading a camera department", "A DP runs a department: operators, assistants, the DIT, the gaffer's and grip's crews. Morale and safety are part of the image.", "Brief the department every morning. Protect the camera crew's breaks. Take responsibility for mistakes publicly.", ["A focus puller misses a critical take.", ["Back them and go again", "talent", "They never miss for you again."], ["Blame them in front of the director", "cha", "The crew goes quiet."]], ""],
    [3, "Shooting for the grade", "Digital cameras record more than the eye sees. A flat log image gives the colourist room; baked-in looks don't.", "Expose to protect highlights. Use a LUT on set so everyone sees the intended look, but record log.", ["The director hates the flat image on the monitor.", ["Apply a show LUT on set", "colour", "Everyone sees the film."], ["Shoot with a heavy look baked in", "colour", "No room later."]], ""],
    [3, "The interview with the director", "DPs are hired in a conversation: how you'd see the film. Your reel gets you the meeting; your ideas get you the job.", "Read the script twice. Bring a few images and one strong idea about the camera's point of view.", ["The director asks how you'd shoot the opening.", ["Describe it from the character's point of view", "vis", "They see the film in your words."], ["List equipment", "comp", "They see a hire company."]], ""],
    [3, "Documentary camera", "Documentary shooting is anticipation: framing a moment before it happens, holding steady when everything is moving, staying invisible.", "Shoot wide enough to catch the unexpected. Learn to listen and frame at the same time.", ["A key moment happens while you're changing batteries.", ["Always keep a spare running", "speed", "You catch it."], ["Ask them to do it again", "eye", "It's never the same."]], ""],
    [3, "Your reel", "A cinematographer's reel shows taste and range in two minutes. The first thirty seconds decide whether anyone watches the rest.", "Lead with your strongest and most distinctive work. Cut to music, but don't let the music carry it.", ["Your best shot is from a student film.", ["Use it", "comp", "Images are images."], ["Use only paid work", "pack", "Weaker, but 'professional'."]], ""]
  ],
  edt: [
    [1, "Logging and organising", "An editor's first job is knowing the footage. Bins, labels and selects decide how fast you can find the moment you need.", "Name clips by scene and take. Build a selects bin for each scene with the best moments marked.", ["You're under pressure and want to start cutting.", ["Organise first", "cont", "You find everything later."], ["Cut straight away", "rhythm", "You lose an hour a day searching."]], ""],
    [1, "The cut on action", "Cutting during movement hides the cut, because the eye follows motion across it.", "Cut a few frames into the action on the outgoing shot and match it on the incoming one.", ["A cut of someone sitting jumps.", ["Cut on the movement of sitting", "cont", "Smooth."], ["Cut after they've sat", "rhythm", "A small jolt."]], ""],
    [1, "J and L cuts", "Letting sound lead or trail the picture across a cut makes conversations flow and pulls the audience into the next scene.", "Bring the next scene's sound in before its picture (J), or let a line run over the cut (L).", ["A dialogue scene feels choppy.", ["Overlap the sound across cuts", "sound", "It flows."], ["Cut every line on the speaker", "rhythm", "Like tennis."]], ""],
    [1, "Reaction shots", "Often the most important shot in a scene is the person listening. Reactions tell the audience how to feel.", "Watch the listener's takes as closely as the speaker's. Cut to reactions at the moment of impact.", ["A big revelation lands flat.", ["Hold on the person hearing it", "shape", "It hits."], ["Stay on the speaker", "rhythm", "Information, no feeling."]], ""],
    [2, "Pacing a scene", "Pace isn't speed. A scene can be cut fast and feel slow because it's repeating information, or slow and feel fast because every beat moves.", "Remove anything the audience already knows. Hold on moments that change something.", ["The producer says the scene is slow.", ["Find the repeated beat and cut it", "shape", "Same length almost, feels faster."], ["Trim every shot", "rhythm", "Shorter, and breathless."]], ""],
    [2, "Montage", "A montage compresses time with images and music. Done well it's a story in miniature; done badly it's a music video.", "Give the montage a beginning, a change and an end. Cut to the music's structure, not every beat.", ["A training montage feels like filler.", ["Show a struggle and a turn", "shape", "Story in two minutes."], ["Add more shots", "rhythm", "Longer filler."]], ""],
    [2, "Temp music", "Temp scores guide cuts and screenings but create 'temp love' that composers then have to fight.", "Use temp sparingly. Choose tracks for function, not because they're great. Warn the director early.", ["The director loves a famous track you can't afford.", ["Swap to temp that does the job but won't be missed", "sound", "The composer has room."], ["Keep it", "rhythm", "The real score always sounds wrong to them."]], ""],
    [2, "Restructuring", "Sometimes the film works better in a different order: an opening moved, a flashback added, a subplot removed.", "Make a scene list on cards. Try orders on the wall before trying them in the timeline.", ["The film doesn't start until twenty minutes in.", ["Move a later scene to the top", "shape", "Gripping from the first frame."], ["Trim the first twenty minutes", "rhythm", "Fifteen minutes before it starts."]], ""],
    [2, "Cutting action", "Action needs clear geography, cause and effect, and rhythm. Fast cutting can hide bad choreography or hide good choreography.", "Establish where everyone is. Let key hits play in wider shots. Use close-ups for effect, not to cover.", ["A fight is confusing.", ["Re-establish geography with a wide", "shape", "Clear and exciting."], ["Cut faster", "rhythm", "Chaos."]], ""],
    [2, "Dialogue editing", "Splitting, cleaning and matching dialogue tracks makes performances clear. Editors pick takes for sound as much as picture.", "Note when a better line reading exists on another take, and steal it if the sync allows.", ["The best picture take has a bad line reading.", ["Swap in the audio from another take", "sound", "Best of both."], ["Use it anyway", "rhythm", "Pretty and wrong."]], ""],
    [3, "The editor's cut", "The editor's cut assembles every scene as scripted. It's long and it's meant to be: the film's raw material laid out.", "Cut everything honestly. Mark what you would lose. Don't pre-judge for the director.", ["The editor's cut is four hours.", ["Show it as is", "shape", "The director sees everything."], ["Quietly trim to two hours", "rhythm", "The director wonders what's missing."]], ""],
    [3, "Working with a director", "The cutting room is intimate and long. Editors who survive are frank in private and loyal in public.", "Say what you think. Try their idea first, fully, before arguing. Never complain about them to the producers.", ["The director insists on a version you think is wrong.", ["Cut it well and show both", "talent", "They choose; you're trusted."], ["Cut it badly to prove your point", "rhythm", "They notice."]], ""],
    [3, "Studio notes and the cut", "Studios have contractual rights to screenings and notes. The editor is often the diplomat between the director and the money.", "Turn notes into options. Show one version that addresses the note and one that doesn't.", ["A studio note will break the ending.", ["Offer a solution to what they're worried about", "pack", "Both camps happy."], ["Do the note", "rhythm", "The ending dies."]], ""],
    [3, "Assistant editors", "Assistants manage media, prep turnovers for sound, VFX and music, and do the work that keeps the editor cutting. Many editors started there.", "Treat assistants as future editors. Let them cut scenes and give them notes.", ["Your assistant asks to cut a scene.", ["Let them, and give honest notes", "talent", "A loyal colleague for life."], ["Too busy", "rhythm", "They leave for another show."]], ""],
    [3, "Turnovers and locked picture", "When picture locks, the editor hands over lists and files to sound, music, VFX and colour. A messy turnover creates months of problems.", "Lock means lock. Prepare clean EDLs, labelled tracks and reference movies.", ["The director wants a small change after lock.", ["Make it and update every department properly", "cont", "Nothing breaks."], ["Make it quietly", "rhythm", "VFX shots no longer line up."]], ""],
    [3, "Trailers versus films", "Trailer editing is its own craft: misdirection, music-driven rhythm, and selling the feeling of a film without spoiling it.", "Find the premise in a line. Cut to music, end on the strongest image or joke.", ["The studio wants the twist in the trailer.", ["Hint at it instead", "mkt", "Intrigue without spoilers."], ["Put it in", "rhythm", "Higher opening, angrier audience."]], ""],
    [3, "Documentary story in the edit", "Documentary is written in the edit. Hundreds of hours become a story, with structure found rather than planned.", "Transcribe everything. Find the characters and the change. Build a paper cut before touching the timeline.", ["You have 300 hours and no story.", ["Find one character with a change", "shape", "A film emerges."], ["Cut the best moments together", "rhythm", "A highlights reel."]], ""],
    [3, "Comedy timing", "Comedy lives in frames. One frame early or late on a reaction and the laugh disappears.", "Cut comedy with an audience in mind. Let reactions breathe. Leave room for laughs at screenings.", ["A joke that killed at the read gets nothing.", ["Adjust the reaction timing by frames", "rhythm", "Big laugh."], ["Cut the joke", "shape", "Safe and less funny."]], ""],
    [3, "The final ten minutes", "The last ten minutes decide how audiences feel walking out. Endings usually play long in the cut.", "Cut the ending as soon as the story is resolved. Hold only on the image that matters most.", ["The film has three endings.", ["Choose one", "shape", "A clean finish."], ["Keep them all", "rhythm", "The audience stands up twice."]], ""],
    [3, "Your rhythm", "Editors develop a recognisable rhythm, as distinctive as a director's eye. Directors hire it.", "Study your own cuts. Notice where you always cut and whether it's habit or choice.", ["A director wants a very different rhythm from yours.", ["Learn theirs", "rhythm", "Range."], ["Cut it your way", "shape", "Your film, not theirs."]], ""],
    [3, "Editing under extreme deadlines", "TV and big releases cut while still shooting, with assemblies due days after the shoot. Speed comes from organisation and decisions.", "Cut every scene the day it arrives. Leave notes on what's missing.", ["The shoot runs late and the network wants a cut on Friday.", ["Use placeholders and flag them", "cont", "On time and clear."], ["Wait for the last footage", "rhythm", "Late."]], ""]
  ],
  pro: [
    [1, "Breaking down a script", "A breakdown lists every element per scene: cast, extras, props, vehicles, effects, wardrobe, locations. It's the base for schedule and budget.", "Use a coloured system per element. Read each scene asking 'what does it need on the day?'.", ["A scene says 'the city burns'.", ["Break it into what's shot and what's VFX", "bud", "A real number."], ["Write 'VFX'", "bud", "A blank cheque."]], ""],
    [1, "Option agreements", "An option buys the right to develop a property for a time, with a price to buy it outright later. It's how producers secure material cheaply.", "Agree the option fee, period, extensions and purchase price. Make sure the rights holder actually owns what they're selling.", ["The author wants a big fee upfront.", ["Offer a small option and a big purchase price", "fin", "Deal done."], ["Pay it", "fin", "Your development money is gone."]], ""],
    [1, "Development hell", "Most projects are developed for years and never made. Producers survive by working on many at once and letting go of dead ones.", "Keep a slate of several projects at different stages. Review them twice a year and drop what isn't moving.", ["A project has been stuck for three years.", ["Let it go", "eye", "Time for what's alive."], ["One more draft", "pack", "Another year."]], ""],
    [1, "The one-page pitch document", "Financiers read one page before anything else: title, logline, comparisons, budget range, who's attached.", "Lead with what makes it sellable. Use comparable films with real numbers. Keep it to a page.", ["Your one-pager is three pages.", ["Cut it to one", "pack", "It gets read."], ["Send it", "mkt", "It gets skimmed."]], ""],
    [2, "Building a budget", "A film budget is above the line (talent, rights) and below the line (crew, equipment, locations, post). Contingency covers what goes wrong.", "Start from the schedule. Price each department. Add contingency of around ten per cent and protect it.", ["The budget is ten per cent over.", ["Cut a day and simplify a set piece", "bud", "On budget."], ["Cut the contingency", "fin", "On budget until something goes wrong."]], ""],
    [2, "Soft money and incentives", "Many countries and states refund a share of local spending. Producers chase incentives to close gaps in finance.", "Compare incentives against crew depth and travel costs. Budget for the cash-flow gap before the rebate pays out.", ["Two locations, one with a bigger rebate.", ["Weigh crew and travel too", "fin", "The real cheapest."], ["Go for the bigger rebate", "fin", "Flying everyone in eats it."]], ""],
    [2, "Pre-sales", "Selling distribution rights in territories before the film is made raises money and proves demand. It depends on cast and genre.", "Attach cast the territories value. Bring a strong package to markets. Expect discounts for the risk.", ["Buyers want a bigger star.", ["Find a star the key territories love", "talent", "Pre-sales close."], ["Wait for an A-lister", "pack", "Two years, still waiting."]], ""],
    [2, "Gap and bridge financing", "When pre-sales and equity don't cover the budget, lenders lend against unsold territories at high interest.", "Know how much is genuinely unsold. Keep the gap small. Read every fee.", ["You're fifteen per cent short a month before shooting.", ["Take gap financing with care", "fin", "The film happens."], ["Delay", "pack", "The cast moves on."]], ""],
    [2, "Completion bonds", "A completion bond guarantees financiers the film will be delivered. Bond companies can take over a production that goes wildly over.", "Budget the fee. Keep the bond company informed. Never surprise them.", ["You're going over budget.", ["Tell the bond company early with a plan", "fin", "They trust you."], ["Hide it", "bud", "They find out, and take over."]], ""],
    [2, "Cast contingencies", "Financing often depends on named actors. If one drops out, money may follow.", "Have back-up cast in mind for every financed role. Check what each financier accepts as a replacement.", ["Your lead drops out weeks before the shoot.", ["Go to the pre-approved list", "talent", "Money holds."], ["Look for someone better", "pack", "The money walks."]], ""],
    [3, "Producing on set", "On set, producers solve problems so the director can work: money, people, time, weather, egos.", "Be on set at key moments, not always. Talk to department heads at lunch. Deal with bad news in private.", ["The director and the star stop speaking.", ["Meet each separately and find a bridge", "talent", "Shooting resumes."], ["Let them sort it out", "pack", "Two days lost."]], ""],
    [3, "Festival strategy", "Choosing a premiere festival shapes a film's sales, reviews and awards. Premiere status can only be spent once.", "Match the film to the festival's audience and buyers. Hire a sales agent and publicist before you land.", ["You have an offer from a smaller festival and hope of a bigger one.", ["Wait for the bigger decision", "dist", "The right launch."], ["Take the bird in the hand", "mkt", "A quieter launch."]], ""],
    [3, "Selling a film", "Sales agents license films to distributors territory by territory. Big festivals bring bidding wars; most films sell slowly.", "Pick an agent with relationships in your genre. Set a minimum. Don't sell every territory on day one.", ["A streamer offers worldwide rights at a modest price.", ["Weigh it against a slower theatrical sale", "dist", "The right deal for the film."], ["Take it immediately", "fin", "Safe, and nobody sees it in a cinema."]], ""],
    [3, "Recoupment and backend", "Profits flow back through a waterfall: distribution fees, expenses, lenders, investors, then profit participants. Many 'hits' never reach the backend.", "Read the waterfall. Negotiate definitions of gross and net. Get audit rights.", ["Your film grossed big and you've seen nothing.", ["Exercise audit rights", "fin", "Money appears."], ["Assume it's normal", "pack", "It is normal. It's also wrong."]], ""],
    [3, "Running a production company", "A company needs overheads, development money and a slate. First-look deals with studios cover costs in exchange for first refusal.", "Keep overheads low. Develop several projects. Negotiate first-look terms that leave you free when they pass.", ["A studio offers a first-look deal with strings.", ["Negotiate the strings down", "pack", "Security and freedom."], ["Sign it", "fin", "Comfortable and stuck."]], ""],
    [3, "Producing television", "Television producers manage seasons, writers' rooms and networks. Showrunners are writer-producers; non-writing producers handle money and production.", "Protect the room's time. Plan episode budgets across the season. Keep the network informed.", ["The network wants a cheaper season.", ["Find savings without cutting what's loved", "bud", "They're pleased."], ["Cut an episode's set piece", "bud", "Fans notice."]], ""],
    [3, "Producing awards campaigns", "Awards campaigns cost millions: screeners, ads, events, consultants. A nomination changes a film's life.", "Choose categories carefully. Target voters with screenings and Q&As. Spend where it moves votes.", ["A small budget for the campaign.", ["Focus on one category with the best chance", "mkt", "A nomination."], ["Spread it across ten", "mkt", "Nothing."]], ""],
    [3, "Ethics and safety", "Producers are responsible for safety on set. Accidents with stunts, firearms and long hours have killed crew members.", "Hire safety advisers. Respect turnaround times. Never pressure a crew to cut corners.", ["The AD wants to skip a safety meeting to save time.", ["Hold it", "eye", "Everyone goes home."], ["Skip it", "bud", "Nothing happens, this time."]], ""],
    [3, "The long game", "Producers' careers are built on relationships over decades. People remember who paid on time and who backed them in a bad week.", "Pay crews promptly. Credit people properly. Keep in touch with collaborators between jobs.", ["A crew member needs a reference.", ["Write a real one", "talent", "A friend for life."], ["Too busy", "pack", "They remember."]], ""]
  ],
  act: [
    [1, "Objectives", "Every character in every scene wants something from someone. Actors play the want, not the feeling.", "Write an active verb for your objective: 'to win her back', 'to make him confess'. Play it at your scene partner.", ["You're asked to play 'sad'.", ["Find what the sadness wants", "range", "Real and specific."], ["Look sad", "pres", "Indicated."]], ""],
    [1, "Listening", "Most screen acting is listening. A face that genuinely hears changes the scene.", "In rehearsal, let your partner's line land before you respond, even a beat. On camera, keep listening when you're off.", ["You're thinking about your next line.", ["Really hear the line", "range", "Your reply surprises you."], ["Hit your line on cue", "pres", "Mechanical."]], ""],
    [1, "Hitting marks", "On screen, actors land on tape marks for focus and lighting without looking down.", "Rehearse the walk and count steps. Use landmarks in the set. Never look at the floor on camera.", ["You miss your mark every take.", ["Count steps in rehearsal", "phys", "Perfect, and invisible."], ["Look down", "pres", "The editor can't use it."]], ""],
    [1, "Scale for camera", "On stage you play to the back row. On screen a close-up shows a thought. Too big reads as false.", "Ask what size the shot is. In a close-up, think it rather than show it.", ["Your performance looks huge in close-up.", ["Bring it inside", "pres", "Riveting."], ["Keep it consistent with the wide", "range", "Shouty."]], ""],
    [1, "The audition", "Auditions are short: often a self-tape or a few minutes in a room. Casting wants to see who you are in the role, not a finished performance.", "Make a clear choice. Know the lines well enough to play. Be pleasant and brief.", ["You're given new pages in the waiting room.", ["Make one strong choice", "impro", "They remember you."], ["Try to memorise every word", "pres", "You're reading, nervous."]], ""],
    [1, "Self-tapes", "Most first auditions are now filmed at home: a plain background, good light, a reader off camera.", "Frame from the chest up. Put the reader beside the lens. Record a few takes and send your best two.", ["Your reader is a flat, monotone friend.", ["Find a better reader", "pres", "Your tape comes alive."], ["Work around it", "range", "It shows."]], ""],
    [1, "Breath and voice", "Voice comes from breath. Tension in the neck and shoulders thins the voice and the performance.", "Warm up daily: breathing low, humming, tongue twisters. Release the jaw.", ["Your voice cracks in an emotional scene.", ["Breathe lower and slower", "voice", "Full and steady."], ["Push harder", "voice", "It cracks again."]], ""],
    [1, "Learning lines", "Lines need to be so known that you can forget them and play. Different actors use different methods.", "Learn thoughts, not words: why each line follows the last. Run them while doing something else.", ["You're word-perfect but stiff.", ["Learn why each line comes next", "range", "Alive."], ["Run them more", "pres", "Faster and stiffer."]], ""],
    [2, "Given circumstances", "Who, where, when, what just happened. Specific circumstances make specific choices.", "Write a page on what happened just before the scene. Play the scene's first line from that.", ["You enter a scene cold.", ["Decide exactly where you've come from", "range", "You enter mid-life."], ["Just start", "pres", "You start at zero."]], ""],
    [2, "Substitution", "Some actors use their own memories to find a feeling; others use imagination. Both are tools, and both need care.", "Choose images that are vivid but not raw. Protect yourself after hard scenes.", ["A scene asks for grief.", ["Use an imagined image", "range", "Real and safe."], ["Dig into your worst memory", "range", "Real, and it costs you."]], ""],
    [2, "Physical character", "How a character stands, walks and holds things tells the audience who they are before they speak.", "Find the centre: head, chest, belly, pelvis. Decide what the character's hands do.", ["Your character is a soldier who hides his fear.", ["Find a rigid centre that cracks", "phys", "We see both."], ["Play the fear", "range", "We only see one."]], ""],
    [2, "Accents and dialects", "Accent work goes beyond sounds: rhythm, placement and attitude. A bad accent distracts from everything.", "Work with a dialect coach. Listen to real speakers. Practise on everyday text before the script.", ["You've two weeks to learn a regional accent.", ["Daily work with a coach", "voice", "Convincing."], ["Watch a film", "lang", "A cartoon."]], ""],
    [2, "Playing comedy", "Comedy is played seriously. The character doesn't know they're funny.", "Commit fully to the character's want. Respect the writer's rhythm. Don't step on laughs.", ["You're trying to be funny.", ["Play it completely straight", "comic", "Huge laugh."], ["Add a funny face", "comic", "Nothing."]], ""],
    [2, "Stage combat", "Fights are choreographed and rehearsed until safe. The actor sells the hit; the partner sells the reaction.", "Learn the moves slowly. Make eye contact before each beat. Never improvise in a fight.", ["Your partner speeds up in the take.", ["Keep your tempo and stay safe", "phys", "Nobody hurt."], ["Match them", "stunt", "Someone gets clipped."]], ""],
    [2, "Intimacy coordination", "Intimate scenes are choreographed like fights, with consent, agreed boundaries and closed sets.", "Agree the choreography with the coordinator. Use barriers. Speak up if anything changes.", ["The director wants to 'see what happens'.", ["Ask for the coordinator", "cha", "Safe and better."], ["Go along with it", "range", "Unsafe and worse."]], ""],
    [2, "Continuity for actors", "Actors match actions across takes and angles so the editor can cut. A cup in the wrong hand ruins a take.", "Repeat business precisely. Check with the script supervisor. Choose simple business.", ["You drink on a different line every take.", ["Fix the business", "cont", "The editor can cut freely."], ["Do it differently each time", "range", "Unusable takes."]], ""],
    [2, "Working with a director", "Directors give notes in different ways. Translating any note into something playable is a skill.", "Turn result notes into actions. Ask one clarifying question, then try it.", ["The director says 'more'.", ["Ask more of what and try it", "range", "The note lands."], ["Play louder", "pres", "Wrong 'more'."]], ""],
    [2, "Green screen and nothing", "Actors often play against nothing: tennis balls on sticks, a stand-in, imagined monsters.", "Get the eyeline exact. Imagine the creature in detail: size, speed, sound. Ask to see concept art.", ["You're terrified of a tennis ball.", ["Ask for the concept art", "range", "Real fear."], ["Imagine something", "pres", "Vague fear."]], ""],
    [3, "Building a character over a series", "Television actors play characters for years, discovering them as the writers do.", "Keep a character bible. Talk to the showrunner about arcs. Protect the character's core.", ["A script makes your character act out of character.", ["Raise it early and kindly", "cha", "A better scene."], ["Play it as written", "range", "Fans complain."]], ""],
    [3, "Leading a cast", "The lead sets the tone for the set: punctuality, preparation, how they treat crew.", "Know everyone's names. Be prepared. Protect younger actors.", ["A supporting actor is struggling.", ["Run lines with them", "cha", "The whole film lifts."], ["Stay in your trailer", "pres", "They struggle on."]], ""],
    [3, "Choosing roles", "A career is shaped as much by what you turn down as what you do. Range and surprise keep you interesting.", "Ask what a role would let you show that you haven't yet. Balance money and growth.", ["A big role like the last three.", ["Take something smaller and different", "range", "The industry sees you anew."], ["Take it", "pres", "Typecast."]], ""],
    [3, "Press and publicity", "Junkets, premieres and interviews sell the film. Actors who handle press well are cast more.", "Have three things you want to say. Be kind to tired journalists. Never trash the film.", ["A journalist asks about a co-star's scandal.", ["Redirect gracefully", "cha", "Good story, no headline."], ["Answer", "pres", "That's the headline."]], ""],
    [3, "Ageing on screen", "Actors age and roles change. The careers that last adapt: character parts, theatre, directing.", "Look for roles that use your age. Keep training. Don't chase the parts you played at thirty.", ["You're offered the father, not the lead.", ["Make it the best father anyone's seen", "range", "A second career."], ["Hold out for leads", "pres", "Fewer offers."]], ""],
    [3, "Stage to screen and back", "Theatre trains stamina, voice and a whole arc in one night. Screen trains stillness and repetition. The best actors move between them.", "Take a stage role every few years. Notice what changes in your screen work after.", ["A theatre offer pays a tenth of a film.", ["Take it", "range", "You come back better."], ["Turn it down", "pres", "Richer, rustier."]], ""],
    [3, "Producing your own work", "Many actors produce their own projects to make the roles they want. It's how many careers change gear.", "Option material you love. Attach yourself. Partner with producers who've done it.", ["Nobody casts you as the lead.", ["Develop a project for yourself", "pack", "You make your own break."], ["Wait to be discovered", "pres", "You wait."]], ""],
    [3, "Recurring nerves", "Even great actors have stage fright. It's managed, not cured.", "Build a pre-show routine. Breathe. Treat the fear as energy.", ["Opening night panic.", ["Use your routine", "com", "The panic becomes fuel."], ["Push through", "pres", "Shaky first act."]], ""],
    [3, "Motion capture", "Mocap actors perform in suits with markers, on bare stages, often playing creatures. The performance drives everything the animators do.", "Commit physically. Understand the creature's anatomy. Exaggerate slightly where the face is hidden.", ["You play a giant ape.", ["Study ape movement for weeks", "phys", "Unforgettable."], ["Wing it", "range", "The animators fix it."]], ""],
    [3, "Voice acting", "Voice actors carry performance through sound alone, often recorded separately from the cast.", "Use your whole body even at the mic. Take direction fast. Vary choices.", ["The director asks for ten versions of a line.", ["Give ten real choices", "voice", "They have options."], ["Give ten similar ones", "voice", "They have one."]], ""],
    [3, "Improvising on camera", "Some directors build scenes from improvisation. It needs listening, generosity and knowing the scene's purpose.", "Know where the scene must end. Say yes to your partner. Stay in character.", ["Your partner improvises a twist.", ["Accept it and build", "impro", "Magic."], ["Steer back to the script", "range", "Safe and flat."]], ""],
    [3, "Sustaining a long run", "Eight shows a week for a year. The challenge is keeping it fresh without changing it.", "Find a new detail each week to notice. Rest your voice. Protect your days off.", ["The show feels mechanical.", ["Refocus on your partner each night", "range", "Fresh."], ["Change your blocking", "pres", "Your partners are thrown."]], ""]
  ]
};
for (const f in CURR_LONG) if (CURR[f]) CURR[f].lessons.push(...CURR_LONG[f]);
